---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import Steps from '../../components/Steps.astro'
import { EnvReplica } from '../../components/app/Replicas'
---
<Base title="Storing a .env - Sanctum guide" description="Import a .env file, keep it byte for byte, and copy one key or the whole file.">
  <section class="section wrap" style="border-top: none;">
    <h2>Storing a .env</h2>
    <p>One record per project and environment. Production, staging and local for the same client sit together in the list.</p>
    <Steps steps={[
      'Open <strong>Env Files</strong> and click <strong>+</strong>.',
      'Name it after the project, and pick the environment.',
      'Click <strong>Import .env</strong> and choose the file, or paste the contents in directly.',
      'Save. What you saved is stored exactly as written.',
    ]} />
    <EnvReplica />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <h2>Why byte for byte matters</h2>
    <p>
      Sanctum stores the raw file text and only parses it to decide what to show you. Comments,
      blank lines, quoting style and key order all survive, so pasting the file back into a fresh
      checkout gives you the file you saved &mdash; not something that resembles it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A store that kept parsed key-value pairs would silently drop your
      <code># rotated 2026-03</code> notes and reorder the rest. It would still &ldquo;work&rdquo;,
      which is what makes that failure mode nasty.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <h2>Copying</h2>
    <p>
      Values are masked until you reveal them, one key at a time. Copy a single value when a client
      rotates one key, or copy the whole file when you are setting up a machine. Both go through the
      same guarded clipboard the Vault uses.
    </p>
    <p>
      A file that will not parse &mdash; anything not <code>KEY=value</code> shaped &mdash; is still
      shown and still copies in full. The parser never decides what gets stored.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
